What is a variable?
A variable is a named memory location that stores a value. he value stored in a variable can change during the execution of a program unless it is declared as final.

Think of it like a labeled box.
Suppose you have a box labeled Age.
+-------------+
| Age         |
|-------------|
| 23          |
+-------------+
Instead of remembering the value 23 everywhere, you simply remember the label Age.

Type of variables
	1. Local Variable:
	
	A local variable is a variable that is declared inside a method, constructor, or block. It can only be used within that method or block. It is created when the method starts executing and Is destroyed when the method finishes. A local variable is stored in the stack memory. Does not get a default value.
	
	Ex: 
	public void test() {
    int age;
    System.out.println(age); // Compilation error
}
	This causes an error because local variables are not automatically initialized.
	
	public class Student {
	public void display() {
        int age = 23;
    }
	public void print() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}
	This gives a compilation error because age belongs only to the display() method.
	
	
	NOTE: Local variables do not receive default values because Java requires programmers to initialize them before use. This helps catch programming errors at compile time and prevents accidental use of uninitialized data.
	
	2. Instance Variable:
	An instance variable is a variable declared inside a class but outside any method, constructor, or block.
	
	public class Student {
	
	    int age = 23;      // Instance variable
	    String name = "Gayathri"; // Instance variable
	
	    public void display() {
	        System.out.println(age);
	        System.out.println(name);
	    }
	}
	
	
	Why is it called an "Instance" Variable?
	Here's the key idea.
	Every time you create an instance (object) of a class, it gets its own copy of the instance variables.
	Example:
	Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();
	Think of it like this:
	Student Class
-------------------
age
name
-------------------
	|
        | creates
        V
	s1
--------
age = 23
name = "Gayathri"
	s2
--------
age = 23
name = "Gayathri"
	Each object has its own separate values.
	If you change:
	s1.age = 30;
	Then:
	s1.age = 30
s2.age = 23
	Changing one object does not affect the other.
	This is why they're called instance variables—they belong to each instance (object).
	
	
	Why do instance variables have default values?
	Remember when we discussed local variables?
	int age;
System.out.println(age); // ❌ Error

	Now look at this:
	public class Student {
	int age;
	public void display() {
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

	This prints: 0
	
	Why?
	Because when you create an object:
	Student s1 = new Student();
	Java allocates memory for the object and automatically initializes all its instance variables with default values.
	Examples:
	int → 0
double → 0.0
boolean → false
char → '\u0000'
String → null
	This ensures every new object starts in a valid, predictable state.
	
	3. Static variable:
	A static variable is a variable declared using the static keyword. It belongs to the class, not to individual objects.
	
	public class Student {
	
	    String name;                 // Instance variable
	    static String college = "RUAS"; // Static variable
	}
	

	Instance Variable	Static Variable
	Belongs to an object	Belongs to the class
	Each object has its own copy	Only one copy exists
	Accessed using an object	Preferably accessed using the class name
	Changes affect only that object	Changes affect all objects
	

